In Java (especially in Streams), map() and flatMap() are used to transform data, but they behave differently.

1️⃣ map()

map() is used when each element maps to exactly one output element.

Example
List<String> names = List.of("Ali", "Ahmed", "Sara");

List<Integer> lengths = names.stream()
        .map(name -> name.length())
        .toList();

System.out.println(lengths);

Output

[3, 5, 4]
Explanation

"Ali" → 3

"Ahmed" → 5

"Sara" → 4

Each input → one output

So the structure does not change.

Stream<String> → Stream<Integer>



2️⃣ flatMap()

flatMap() is used when each element produces multiple elements (a stream or collection).

It flattens nested structures.

Example
List<List<String>> names = List.of(
        List.of("Ali", "Ahmed"),
        List.of("Sara", "John")
);

List<String> result = names.stream()
        .flatMap(list -> list.stream())
        .toList();

System.out.println(result);

Output

[Ali, Ahmed, Sara, John]
Explanation

Original structure:

[
 [Ali, Ahmed],
 [Sara, John]
]

flatMap() flattens it into one stream

[Ali, Ahmed, Sara, John]


🔑 Key Difference
| Feature            | map()           | flatMap()                      |
| ------------------ | --------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Output per element | One             | Many                           |
| Structure          | Same            | Flattened                      |
| Used when          | Transform value | Flatten nested collections     |
| Return type        | `Stream<T>`     | `Stream<T>` (after flattening) |

📌 Simple Interview Definition

map()

Used to transform each element of a stream into another element.

flatMap()

Used to transform each element into a stream and then flatten all streams into one stream.

🔥 Real Backend Example (Very Common Interview Question)
Using map()
List<User> users;

List<String> names = users.stream()
        .map(User::getName)
        .toList();
Using flatMap()

If a user has multiple phone numbers

List<String> phones = users.stream()
        .flatMap(user -> user.getPhones().stream())
        .toList();

This collects all phone numbers from all users.

✅ Simple memory trick

map()     → One to One
flatMap() → One to Many + Flatten